<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/SimonCan/BlenDaViz">BlenDaViz</ext-link> (<xref alt="Candelaresi &amp; Smiet, 2026" rid="ref-candelaresi_2026_23810340" ref-type="bibr">Candelaresi
  &amp; Smiet, 2026</xref>) is a 3D plotting library for the
  visualization tool
  Blender (<xref alt="Blender Development Team, 2025" rid="ref-blender" ref-type="bibr">Blender
  Development Team, 2025</xref>), written in Python. It is able to
  visualize line plots, scatter plots, surfaces, iso-surfaces, glyphs
  (arrows) and streamlines from
  NumPy (<xref alt="Harris et al., 2020" rid="ref-harris2020" ref-type="bibr">Harris
  et al., 2020</xref>) arrays. The user can load BlenDaViz within a
  Blender Python console as any other library. Its design is close to
  other plotting libraries, especially
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://matplotlib.org">Matplotlib</ext-link> (<xref alt="Hunter, 2007" rid="ref-Hunter2007" ref-type="bibr">Hunter,
  2007</xref>;
  <xref alt="Matplotlib Development Team, 2026" rid="ref-matplotlib" ref-type="bibr">Matplotlib
  Development Team, 2026</xref>), which means that every part of a plot
  is treated as an object that can be changed when updating the plot
  with new parameters or data. Compared to other 3D visualization
  software, we achieve higher image quality, thanks to Blender’s ray
  tracing capabilities.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p>In scientific fields, like structural mechanics, computational
  fluid dynamics, astrophysics, or molecular dynamics, it is common to
  use 3D visualization tools like
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.paraview.org">ParaView</ext-link> (<xref alt="Ahrens et al., 2005" rid="ref-Ahrens2005" ref-type="bibr">Ahrens
  et al., 2005</xref>),
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.vapor.ucar.edu">VAPOR</ext-link> (<xref alt="Gruchalla et al., 2009" rid="ref-vapor" ref-type="bibr">Gruchalla
  et al., 2009</xref>),
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://pypi.org/project/mayavi">Mayavi</ext-link> (<xref alt="Ramachandran &amp; Varoquaux, 2011" rid="ref-ramachandran2011mayavi" ref-type="bibr">Ramachandran
  &amp; Varoquaux, 2011</xref>) or
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://wci.llnl.gov/simulation/computer-codes/visit">VisIt</ext-link> (<xref alt="Childs et al., 2012" rid="ref-childs2012visit" ref-type="bibr">Childs
  et al., 2012</xref>). They can load a variety of data formats and
  visualize different aspects of the data using filters. However, they
  often lack the image quality needed to make the data easier to
  understand. Their limited shaders can create optical illusions where
  the depth of the geometry is not clear, or the colors are off due to
  unrealistic image generation.</p>
  <p>Realistically rendered or ray-traced images, like those generated
  by 3D modelling tools like
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.blender.org">Blender</ext-link>,
  not only improve the image quality, but make it easier to understand
  the three-dimensional structure and colors. For instance, contextually
  implied scattered light can have a significant impact on the perceived
  color of objects, as exemplified by <italic>The Dress</italic>
  controversy (<xref alt="Chetverikov &amp; Ivanchei, 2016" rid="ref-chetverikov2016seeing" ref-type="bibr">Chetverikov
  &amp; Ivanchei, 2016</xref>). Lighting, shadow, depth and scattering
  can be used to generate photorealistic images that are easier to
  interpret.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the field</title>
  <p>So far Blender has seen only limited use in 3D data visualization.
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.bioblender.org">BioBlender</ext-link> (<xref alt="Andrei et al., 2012" rid="ref-andrei2012" ref-type="bibr">Andrei
  et al., 2012</xref>) has been developed specifically for biological
  data and requires significant Blender knowledge. Similarly,
  suggestions to use Blender in
  astrophysics (<xref alt="Kent, 2015" rid="ref-Kent-2015" ref-type="bibr">Kent,
  2015</xref>) require the generation of the geometry by hand.
  High-quality plots by the
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.olcf.ornl.gov/2011/08/03/ornl-animations-garner-peer-praise-and-prizes/">Oak
  Ridge National Laboratory</ext-link> show that to achieve high quality
  a collaboration between Blender experts and scientists is sometimes
  required.</p>
  <p>Attempts at creating libraries were made with
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.astroblend.com">AstroBlend</ext-link> (<xref alt="Naiman, 2016" rid="ref-NAIMAN201650" ref-type="bibr">Naiman,
  2016</xref>) and
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/zeffii/BlenderSciViz">BlenderSciViz</ext-link>,
  but their applications are relatively limited and, as of November
  2026, neither appears to be maintained: the last commit to
  BlenderSciViz dates from 24 December 2015 (commit 51535b3), while the
  AstroBlend documentation covers Blender versions up to 2.76b (2015)
  and its source repository is no longer available. Perhaps
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/simboden/BVtkNodes">BVtkNodes</ext-link>
  and
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://extensions.blender.org/add-ons/sciblend/">SciBlend</ext-link> (<xref alt="Marín et al., 2025" rid="ref-SciBlend" ref-type="bibr">Marín
  et al., 2025</xref>) stand out, as they are able to load VTK data and
  apply filters to it.</p>
  <p>With BlenDaViz we give the users all the rendering and ray tracing
  capabilities of Blender, combined with the ease of use and modularity
  we know from libraries like Matplotlib. It is general purpose, thanks
  to the implementation of some of the most used 3D plotting
  methods.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software design</title>
  <p>With BlenDaViz we keep the design very close to Matplotlib. The
  user supplies the input data in the form of NumPy arrays and the
  resulting plots are objects that can be freely modified. These objects
  contain members, such as the data and parameters (e.g. color), and
  methods, like the plotting function. That way, the user can modify
  their plots and replot them. An example is shown here where we
  generate a simple line plot, change the data of the resulting plot
  object and replot it:</p>
  <code language="python">import blendaviz as blt
import numpy as np

# Generate the data.
y = np.linspace(0, 6*np.pi, 400)
x = 2*np.cos(y)
z = 2*np.sin(y)

# Generate the line plot.
pl = blt.plot(x, y, z, radius=0.5)

# Change the data.
pl.x = 2*pl.x

# Replot the data.
pl.plot()</code>
  <p>Each of our plotting functions follows a similar pipeline. As the
  plotting function is called, the input is checked for consistency.
  Then any existing geometry or materials for this plot are deleted.
  Depending on the plotting function, different kinds of meshes are
  generated (lines, surfaces, arrows, etc.). To finalize the plot object
  a material is added. Geometry and material are then part of the
  plotting object and can, in principle, be accessed. After the geometry
  generation we add lights and a camera, if not already present.</p>
  <p>An example is shown in
  <xref alt="[fig: mesh_plot]" rid="figU003AU0020mesh_plot">[fig: mesh_plot]</xref>
  where we plot a two-dimensional array as a surface with the values
  used as <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[z]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>z</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>-coordinates
  and as colors. To highlight its geometry we added a plane with a
  checkerboard texture in the background and readjusted the camera. Such
  manual adjustments can be done directly in Blender and can greatly
  improve the plot quality.</p>
  <fig>
    <caption><p>Mesh plot of a Gaussian using a
    colormap.<styled-content id="figU003AU0020mesh_plot"></styled-content></p></caption>
    <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="mesh_plot.png" />
  </fig>
</sec>
<sec id="handling-of-time">
  <title>Handling of time</title>
  <p>We make handling time-dependent data easy in BlenDaViz. All that is
  required is that the data arrays contain one more dimension. For
  instance, if we want to plot the position of
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[n]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  particles at <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[p]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>p</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  different times, our arrays <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[x]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>x</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[y]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>y</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[z]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>z</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  would have the shape <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[[n, p]]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">[</mml:mo><mml:mi>n</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>p</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">]</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>.
  Parameters that take the same shape, like colors, follow the same
  structure.</p>
  <p>BlenDaViz recognizes the data and input parameters as time
  dependent. It then adds the plotting method of this plot object to the
  <monospace>bpy.app.handlers.frame_change_pre</monospace> handler of
  Blender. That way, any change in the time via, e.g. the slider,
  results in an update of all present plots.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p>In Candelaresi et al.
  (<xref alt="2021" rid="ref-vortex_braids" ref-type="bibr">2021</xref>)
  BlenDaViz was used to visualize the vortex field of a turbulent
  finite-difference simulation using the Pencil
  Code (<xref alt="The Pencil Code Collaboration et al., 2021" rid="ref-PencilCodeCollaboration-2021-58-2807-JOpSoSoft" ref-type="bibr">The
  Pencil Code Collaboration et al., 2021</xref>). Later Israeli et al.
  (<xref alt="2023" rid="ref-israeli2023euv" ref-type="bibr">2023</xref>)
  and Candelaresi &amp; Beck
  (<xref alt="2023" rid="ref-TwistedFields2023" ref-type="bibr">2023</xref>)
  used it to visualize magnetic field configurations obtained from
  simulations. The former also uses BlenDaViz to present particle
  trajectories.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI usage disclosure</title>
  <p>We used Claude Sonnet 4.6 for some code debugging and to help write
  the majority of tests. All changes made by Claude were verified by
  hand.</p>
</sec>
